<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Sites change with the calendar. A store shows a sale banner for one week, a news site switches to a
darker look in the evening, a game adds snow in December, and a product page reveals its "Buy now" button
at the moment of launch. Today each of these needs script that reads the clock, compares dates, and
toggles a class, and then has to run again when the date changes while the page is open.

This specification lets a style sheet ask about the date directly, the same way it asks about the width
of the viewport. Dates and times are written as ISO 8601 strings, in exactly the formats that
Temporal [[!TEMPORAL]] parses, and they compare the way Temporal compares them.

<div class=example id=example-intro>
    <pre highlight=css>
    /* A sale banner from September 9 through September 15, 2026 */
    @media ("2026-09-09" <= date <= "2026-09-15") {
      .sale-banner { display: block; }
    }

    /* Snow every year from December 20 through January 5 */
    @media (date >= "--12-20"), (date <= "--01-05") {
      body { background-image: url(snow.svg); }
    }

    /* Warmer colors from 6pm local time */
    @media (time >= "18:00") {
      :root { --accent: orange; }
    }

    /* "Buy now" appears at 9:00 in New York, the same moment everywhere */
    @media (date >= "2026-11-27T09:00[America/New_York]") {
      .buy-now { display: inline-block; }
    }

    /* US Thanksgiving, and Passover in the Hebrew calendar */
    @media holiday(us, thanksgiving) { … }
    @media calendar(hebrew, (month: M07) and (15 <= day <= 22)) { … }

    /* Full moon, and winter in whichever hemisphere the user is in */
    @media (moon-phase: full-moon) { … }
    @media (season: winter) { … }
    </pre>
</div>

Because these are media queries, they also work in HTML's <code>media</code> attributes and in
<code>matchMedia()</code>, and they update on their own when the date changes.

<div class=example id=example-intro-html>
    <pre highlight=html>
    &lt;picture>
      &lt;source media='(date = "--12-25")' srcset="hero-christmas.jpg">
      &lt;img src="hero.jpg" alt="">
    &lt;/picture>
    </pre>

    CSS strings can use single or double quotes, so a query inside an HTML attribute uses whichever
    quote the attribute doesn't.
</div>


<h2 id=syntax>Syntax</h2>

This specification makes two changes to the media query grammar in [[!MEDIAQUERIES-5]].

1. <<mf-value>> gains a <<string>> alternative, so a media feature can be compared with a string:

    <pre class=prod>
    <<mf-value>> = <<number>> | <<dimension>> | <<ident>> | <<ratio>> | <<string>>
    </pre>

    The media features in [[!MEDIAQUERIES-5]] don't accept strings, so this changes nothing for them.

2. <<media-in-parens>> gains the ''holiday()'' and ''calendar()'' functions:

    <pre class=prod>
    <<media-in-parens>> = ( <<media-condition>> ) | ( <<media-feature>> )
                        | <<holiday()>> | <<calendar()>> | <<general-enclosed>>
    </pre>

    As before, the <<general-enclosed>> branch is chosen only if the input matches none of the others.

<div class=note>
    A user agent that doesn't implement this specification parses every query in it as
    <<general-enclosed>>, or as an unknown <<media-feature>>. Both evaluate to "unknown", which makes
    the media query false. Styles meant for a date are never applied early by an older browser; they
    are not applied at all.
</div>


<h2 id=now>The current date and time</h2>

The <dfn export>current instant</dfn> is [$SystemUTCEpochNanoseconds$](), rounded down to a whole
second (a multiple of 10<sup>9</sup> nanoseconds).

The <dfn export>current time zone</dfn> is [$SystemTimeZoneIdentifier$]().

The <dfn export>current date-time</dfn> is the [=ISO Date-Time Record=] returned by
[$GetISODateTimeFor$]([=current time zone=], [=current instant=]). Its \[[ISODate]] field is the
<dfn export>current date</dfn>, and its \[[Time]] field is the <dfn export>current time</dfn>.

<div class=note>
    These are the values that <code>Temporal.Now.zonedDateTimeISO()</code> returns in script, except
    for the rounding. A document's styles and its scripts agree about what day it is.
</div>

When the user agent evaluates media queries for a document, it uses one [=current instant=] for all of
them, so two queries about the same moment never disagree.

The media features and functions in this specification change value as time passes, and when the
[=current time zone=] or the system clock changes. When that changes the result of a media query, the
user agent must respond as it does when any other media feature changes: it restyles the document and
fires <code>change</code> events at the affected <code>MediaQueryList</code> objects. It should do so
within one second.

<div class=note>
    Implementations don't need to poll. From the queries in use, a user agent can work out the next
    instant at which any result can change, such as a boundary written in a query or the next local
    midnight, and wait until then.
</div>


<h2 id=strings>Date and time strings</h2>

<h3 id=date-strings>Date strings</h3>

A <dfn type>&lt;date-string></dfn> is a <<string>> for which [=parse a date string=] doesn't return
failure.

To <dfn export>parse a date string</dfn> |s|, run these steps. If any operation they call throws an
exception, return failure.

1. If [$ParseText$](|s|, [=TemporalDateTimeString=]<sub>[+Zoned]</sub>) is a Parse Node, the string
    names a time zone. Return an <dfn>instant value</dfn>: the \[[EpochNanoseconds]] of
    [$ToTemporalZonedDateTime$](|s|).
1. If [$ParseText$](|s|, [=TemporalInstantString=]) is a Parse Node, the string has a UTC offset. Return
    an [=instant value=]: the \[[EpochNanoseconds]] of [$ToTemporalInstant$](|s|).
1. If [$ParseText$](|s|, [=TemporalDateTimeString=]<sub>[~Zoned]</sub>) is a Parse Node:
    1. Let |result| be [$ParseISODateTime$](|s|, « [=TemporalDateTimeString=]<sub>[~Zoned]</sub> »).
    1. If |result|.\[[Calendar]] is not empty, perform
        [$CanonicalizeCalendar$](|result|.\[[Calendar]]).
    1. Let |date| be the [=ISO Date Record=] with \[[Year]] |result|.\[[Year]], \[[Month]]
        |result|.\[[Month]], and \[[Day]] |result|.\[[Day]].
    1. If [$ISODateWithinLimits$](|date|) is false, return failure.
    1. If |result|.\[[Time]] is start-of-day, the string has no time. Return a
        <dfn>date value</dfn>: |date|.
    1. Let |dateTime| be [$CombineISODateAndTimeRecord$](|date|, |result|.\[[Time]]).
    1. If [$ISODateTimeWithinLimits$](|dateTime|) is false, return failure.
    1. Return a <dfn>date-time value</dfn>: |dateTime|.
1. If [$ParseText$](|s|, [=AnnotatedMonthDay=]) is a Parse Node:
    1. Let |result| be [$ParseISODateTime$](|s|, « [=TemporalMonthDayString=] »).
    1. Return a <dfn>month-day value</dfn>: the pair (|result|.\[[Month]], |result|.\[[Day]]).
1. Return failure.

A calendar annotation such as <code>[u-ca=hebrew]</code> is accepted, as Temporal accepts it, but it
doesn't change the date: the fields of the string are always ISO 8601 fields. To match dates in another
calendar, use ''calendar()''.

To <dfn export>compare the current date with</dfn> a parsed date string |v|:

: If |v| is an [=instant value=]
:: Return [$CompareEpochNanoseconds$]([=current instant=], |v|).
: If |v| is a [=date-time value=]
:: Return [$CompareISODateTime$]([=current date-time=], |v|).
: If |v| is a [=date value=]
:: Return [$CompareISODate$]([=current date=], |v|).
: If |v| is a [=month-day value=] (|month|, |day|)
:: Return [$CompareISODate$](|a|, |b|), where |a| is the [=ISO Date Record=] for year 1972 with the
    [=current date=]'s month and day, and |b| is the one for year 1972 with |month| and |day|.

The result is -1 if the current date comes first, 0 if they are equal, and 1 if |v| comes first.

<div class=note>
    1972 is the reference year that Temporal uses for <code>PlainMonthDay</code>. It is a leap year,
    so <code>"--02-29"</code> is a valid month-day. In other years no date is equal to it, and March 1
    is after it.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-date-strings>
    <table class=data>
        <thead><tr><th>String<th>Kind<th>Meaning
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>"2026-09-09"</code><td>date<td>September 9, 2026, in the user's time zone
            <tr><td><code>"20260909"</code><td>date<td>The same date, in ISO 8601 basic format
            <tr><td><code>"2026-11-27T09:00"</code><td>date-time<td>9:00 on November 27, 2026, in the user's time zone
            <tr><td><code>"2026-11-27T09:00[America/New_York]"</code><td>instant<td>9:00 on that day in New York, one moment for every user
            <tr><td><code>"2026-11-27T14:00Z"</code><td>instant<td>The same moment, written in UTC
            <tr><td><code>"2026-11-27[America/New_York]"</code><td>instant<td>The start of that day in New York
            <tr><td><code>"--12-25"</code> or <code>"12-25"</code><td>month-day<td>December 25 of every year
    </table>
</div>

<h3 id=time-strings>Time strings</h3>

A <dfn type>&lt;time-string></dfn> is a <<string>> for which [=parse a time string=] doesn't return
failure.

To <dfn export>parse a time string</dfn> |s|:

1. If |s| doesn't start with "T" or "t", set |s| to the concatenation of "T" and |s|.
1. If [$ParseText$](|s|, [=TimeDesignator=] [=Time=]) is not a Parse Node, return failure.
1. Return the \[[Time]] of [$ToTemporalTime$](|s|). If that throws, return failure.

<div class=note>
    Adding the "T" prevents a basic-format time such as <code>"1225"</code> from being read as a
    month and day, which is how Temporal resolves the same ambiguity.
</div>


<h2 id=media-features>Media features</h2>

All of these features have a value at all times, so in a [=boolean context=] they are always true.
Range features also accept the <code>min-</code> and <code>max-</code> prefixes, as
[[!MEDIAQUERIES-5]] defines for every range feature.

<h3 id=date-feature>The '@media/date' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: date
Value: <<date-string>>
For: @media
Type: range
</pre>

The '@media/date' media feature is the current date and time. Compared with a <<date-string>> |v|, the
feature is less than, equal to, or greater than |v| when the result of
[=compare the current date with|comparing the current date with=] |v| is -1, 0, or 1.

Each comparison in a query parses its own string, so a range can mix kinds:
<code>("2026-09-09" <= date < "2026-09-15T12:00")</code> starts at the beginning of September 9 and
ends at noon on September 15.

<div class=example id=example-date-wrap>
    A range of month-days doesn't wrap around the end of the year, because
    <code>("--12-20" <= date <= "--01-05")</code> means
    <code>(date >= "--12-20") and (date <= "--01-05")</code>, which no date satisfies. Join the two
    halves with a comma or <code>or</code> instead:

    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (date >= "--12-20") or (date <= "--01-05") { … }
    </pre>
</div>

<h3 id=time-feature>The '@media/time' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: time
Value: <<time-string>>
For: @media
Type: range
</pre>

The '@media/time' media feature is the [=current time=]. Compared with a <<time-string>>, whose parsed
value is |t|, the feature is less than, equal to, or greater than it when
[$CompareTimeRecord$]([=current time=], |t|) is -1, 0, or 1.

<div class=example id=example-time-night>
    <pre highlight=css>
    /* From 10pm to 6am */
    @media (time >= "22:00") or (time < "06:00") { … }
    </pre>
</div>

<h3 id=weekday-feature>The '@media/weekday' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: weekday
Value: monday | tuesday | wednesday | thursday | friday | saturday | sunday
For: @media
Type: range
</pre>

The '@media/weekday' media feature is the day of the week of the [=current date=]. The keywords stand
for the numbers 1 (''monday'') through 7 (''sunday''), the numbering of [$ISODayOfWeek$], and compare
as those numbers. The value is [$ISODayOfWeek$]([=current date=]).

<div class=example id=example-weekend>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (weekday >= saturday) { .weekend-hours { display: block; } }
    </pre>
</div>

<h3 id=month-feature>The '@media/month' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: month
Value: <<integer [1,99]>> | <<month-code>>
For: @media
Type: range
</pre>

A <dfn type>&lt;month-code></dfn> is an <<ident>> that, converted to ASCII uppercase, is matched by
the [=MonthCode=] production of [[!TEMPORAL]], such as ''M01'' or ''M05L''. An <<integer>> |n| is
equivalent to the [=month code=] [$CreateMonthCode$](|n|, false), so ''12'' means ''M12''.

The '@media/month' media feature is the \[[MonthCode]] of
[$CalendarISOToDate$]([=date calendar=], [=current date=]). Month codes compare by code unit order,
which [[!TEMPORAL]] defines to be the order of the months in a year, including leap months.

<h3 id=day-feature>The '@media/day' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: day
Value: <<integer [1,∞]>>
For: @media
Type: range
</pre>

The '@media/day' media feature is the \[[Day]] of
[$CalendarISOToDate$]([=date calendar=], [=current date=]): the day of the month.

<div class=example id=example-nth-weekday>
    '@media/month', '@media/day', and '@media/weekday' together describe any "<var>n</var>th weekday of the month" rule,
    because the fourth Thursday of a month always falls on day 22 to 28, and the last Monday of May on
    day 25 to 31:

    <pre highlight=css>
    @media (month: 11) and (weekday: thursday) and (22 <= day <= 28) { /* US Thanksgiving */ }
    @media (month: 5) and (weekday: monday) and (day >= 25) { /* US Memorial Day */ }
    </pre>
</div>

<h3 id=moon-phase-feature>The '@media/moon-phase' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: moon-phase
Value: new-moon | waxing-crescent | first-quarter | waxing-gibbous | full-moon | waning-gibbous | last-quarter | waning-crescent
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The <dfn>principal phases</dfn> of the Moon are the instants at which the Moon's geocentric apparent
ecliptic longitude exceeds the Sun's by 0° (''new-moon''), 90° (''first-quarter''), 180°
(''full-moon''), and 270° (''last-quarter''). The date of a principal phase is the \[[ISODate]] of
[$GetISODateTimeFor$]([=current time zone=], the phase's instant).

The '@media/moon-phase' media feature is:

* The principal phase whose date is the [=current date=], if there is one.
* Otherwise, the phase after the most recent of the [=principal phases=]: ''waxing-crescent'' after ''new-moon'',
    ''waxing-gibbous'' after ''first-quarter'', ''waning-gibbous'' after ''full-moon'', and
    ''waning-crescent'' after ''last-quarter''.

User agents must place the instants of the principal phases within one minute of their true times.

<div class=note>
    The method in chapter 49 of [[MEEUS]] is accurate enough. Principal phases are about seven days
    apart, so no date has two of them. Because the date depends on the time zone, users in different
    time zones can see a full moon on different dates.
</div>

<h3 id=season-feature>The '@media/season' feature</h3>

<pre class='descdef mq'>
Name: season
Value: spring | summer | autumn | winter
For: @media
Type: discrete
</pre>

The astronomical seasons are bounded by the <dfn>March equinox</dfn>, <dfn>June solstice</dfn>,
<dfn>September equinox</dfn>, and <dfn>December solstice</dfn>: the instants at which the Sun's
geocentric apparent ecliptic longitude is 0°, 90°, 180°, and 270°. The date of each is the \[[ISODate]]
of [$GetISODateTimeFor$]([=current time zone=], its instant) in the year of the [=current date=].

The <dfn>hemisphere</dfn> is southern if the [=current time zone=] is a named time zone whose location
in the Time Zone Database [[!TZDB]] has a negative latitude, and northern otherwise. Offset time zones
and zones with no location, such as "UTC", are northern. User agents use the entry for the time zone's
own identifier where the database lists one, and otherwise the entry for the zone it links to.

The '@media/season' media feature is found from the [=current date=] |d|:

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>When |d| is<th>Northern<th>Southern
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>on or after the date of the [=March equinox=] and before the date of the [=June solstice=]<td>''spring''<td>''autumn''
        <tr><td>on or after the date of the [=June solstice=] and before the date of the [=September equinox=]<td>''summer''<td>''winter''
        <tr><td>on or after the date of the [=September equinox=] and before the date of the [=December solstice=]<td>''autumn''<td>''spring''
        <tr><td>any other date<td>''winter''<td>''summer''
</table>

User agents must place the equinoxes and solstices within one minute of their true times.

<div class=note>
    The method in chapter 27 of [[MEEUS]] is accurate enough for dates near the present. The hemisphere
    comes from the time zone, which script can already read, rather than from the user's location.
    Near the equator the astronomical seasons don't match the local climate, and authors should keep
    that in mind for audiences there.
</div>


<h2 id=functions>Functions</h2>

<h3 id=calendar-function>The ''calendar()'' function</h3>

<pre class=prod>
<dfn type>&lt;calendar()></dfn> = calendar( <<ident>> , <<media-condition>> )
</pre>

The <dfn function>calendar()</dfn> function evaluates a media condition in another calendar.

Every [=media condition=] is evaluated with a <dfn>date calendar</dfn>, which is "iso8601" unless
''calendar()'' says otherwise. The date calendar decides what '@media/month' and '@media/day' mean. It
doesn't affect '@media/date', '@media/time', or '@media/weekday', which are the same in every calendar.

To evaluate <code>calendar(|name|, |condition|)</code>:

1. Let |calendar| be [$CanonicalizeCalendar$](|name|). If that throws, because the user agent doesn't
    support a calendar of that [=calendar type=], the result is "unknown".
1. The result is the result of evaluating |condition| with |calendar| as the [=date calendar=].

The calendar types are the ones <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code> supports, such as ''hebrew'',
''chinese'', ''islamic-umalqura'', ''persian'', and ''ethiopic''.

<div class=example id=example-calendar>
    <pre highlight=css>
    /* Lunar New Year through the Lantern Festival */
    @media calendar(chinese, (month: M01) and (day <= 15)) { … }

    /* Purim: 14 Adar, which is Adar II in a Hebrew leap year */
    @media calendar(hebrew, (month: M06) and (day = 14)) { … }
    </pre>
</div>

<h3 id=holiday-function>The ''holiday()'' function</h3>

<pre class=prod>
<dfn type>&lt;holiday()></dfn> = holiday( [ <<ident>> , ]? <<ident>> )
</pre>

The <dfn function>holiday()</dfn> function tests whether the [=current date=] is a holiday.
Its last argument names the holiday. The optional first argument is a region: two ASCII letters, an ISO
3166-1 alpha-2 code as used in Unicode region subtags, such as ''us'' or ''il''. The author chooses the
region; the user's own region plays no part.

To evaluate <code>holiday(|region|, |name|)</code>, with |region| possibly omitted:

1. If |region| is present and isn't two ASCII letters, the result is "unknown".
1. Let |entry| be the entry of the [[#holiday-registry]] whose name matches |name| and whose region
    matches |region|, ignoring ASCII case.
1. If there is no such entry, let |entry| be the entry whose name matches |name| and that has no region.
1. If there is still no entry, the result is "unknown".
1. The result is true if the [=current date=] is a date of |entry|, and false otherwise.

The dates of an entry are the dates on which its rule's [=media condition=] is true, evaluated with
"iso8601" as the [=date calendar=], or the dates its rule describes in words. If an entry's rule uses a
calendar that the user agent doesn't support, the result is "unknown".

<div class=example id=example-holiday>
    <pre highlight=css>
    @media holiday(us, thanksgiving) { … }  /* fourth Thursday of November */
    @media holiday(ca, thanksgiving) { … }  /* second Monday of October */
    @media holiday(us, christmas) { … }     /* no US entry, so the regionless one */
    @media holiday(il, passover) { … }      /* 7 days in Israel, instead of 8 */
    </pre>
</div>

<h3 id=holiday-registry>Holiday registry</h3>

<table class=data>
    <thead><tr><th>Name<th>Region<th>Rule
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>''new-years-day''<td><td><code>(date = "--01-01")</code>
        <tr><td>''valentines-day''<td><td><code>(date = "--02-14")</code>
        <tr><td>''halloween''<td><td><code>(date = "--10-31")</code>
        <tr><td>''christmas-eve''<td><td><code>(date = "--12-24")</code>
        <tr><td>''christmas''<td><td><code>(date = "--12-25")</code>
        <tr><td>''new-years-eve''<td><td><code>(date = "--12-31")</code>
        <tr><td>''easter''<td><td>The [=Gregorian Easter=] of the year of the [=current date=]
        <tr><td>''good-friday''<td><td>2 days before ''easter''
        <tr><td>''orthodox-easter''<td><td>The [=Orthodox Easter=] of the year of the [=current date=]
        <tr><td>''lunar-new-year''<td><td><code>calendar(chinese, (month: M01) and (day = 1))</code>
        <tr><td>''mid-autumn-festival''<td><td><code>calendar(chinese, (month: M08) and (day = 15))</code>
        <tr><td>''rosh-hashanah''<td><td><code>calendar(hebrew, (month: M01) and (day <= 2))</code>
        <tr><td>''yom-kippur''<td><td><code>calendar(hebrew, (month: M01) and (day = 10))</code>
        <tr><td>''passover''<td><td><code>calendar(hebrew, (month: M07) and (15 <= day <= 22))</code>
        <tr><td>''passover''<td>''il''<td><code>calendar(hebrew, (month: M07) and (15 <= day <= 21))</code>
        <tr><td>''hanukkah''<td><td>The 8 days starting on each date whose \[[MonthCode]] and \[[Day]] in
            [$CalendarISOToDate$]("hebrew", date) are "M03" and 25 (25 Kislev)
        <tr><td>''thanksgiving''<td>''us''<td><code>(month: 11) and (weekday: thursday) and (22 <= day <= 28)</code>
        <tr><td>''independence-day''<td>''us''<td><code>(date = "--07-04")</code>
        <tr><td>''memorial-day''<td>''us''<td><code>(month: 5) and (weekday: monday) and (day >= 25)</code>
        <tr><td>''labor-day''<td>''us''<td><code>(month: 9) and (weekday: monday) and (day <= 7)</code>
        <tr><td>''mothers-day''<td>''us''<td><code>(month: 5) and (weekday: sunday) and (8 <= day <= 14)</code>
        <tr><td>''fathers-day''<td>''us''<td><code>(month: 6) and (weekday: sunday) and (15 <= day <= 21)</code>
        <tr><td>''thanksgiving''<td>''ca''<td><code>(month: 10) and (weekday: monday) and (8 <= day <= 14)</code>
        <tr><td>''canada-day''<td>''ca''<td><code>(date = "--07-01")</code>
        <tr><td>''mothers-day''<td>''gb''<td>21 days before ''easter''
        <tr><td>''dia-de-muertos''<td>''mx''<td><code>("--11-01" <= date <= "--11-02")</code>
</table>

The <dfn>Gregorian Easter</dfn> of year |Y| is found with integer division and a non-negative remainder:

1. Let |a| be |Y| mod 19, |b| be |Y| ÷ 100, and |c| be |Y| mod 100.
1. Let |d| be |b| ÷ 4, |e| be |b| mod 4, |f| be (|b| + 8) ÷ 25, and |g| be (|b| − |f| + 1) ÷ 3.
1. Let |h| be (19|a| + |b| − |d| − |g| + 15) mod 30.
1. Let |i| be |c| ÷ 4, and |k| be |c| mod 4.
1. Let |l| be (32 + 2|e| + 2|i| − |h| − |k|) mod 7.
1. Let |m| be (|a| + 11|h| + 22|l|) ÷ 451.
1. Let |n| be |h| + |l| − 7|m| + 114.
1. Return the ISO date in year |Y|, month |n| ÷ 31, day (|n| mod 31) + 1.

The <dfn>Orthodox Easter</dfn> of year |Y| is found the same way:

1. Let |a| be |Y| mod 4, |b| be |Y| mod 7, and |c| be |Y| mod 19.
1. Let |d| be (19|c| + 15) mod 30.
1. Let |e| be (2|a| + 4|b| − |d| + 34) mod 7.
1. Let |n| be |d| + |e| + 114.
1. Let |julian| be the date in year |Y|, month |n| ÷ 31, day (|n| mod 31) + 1, in the Julian calendar.
1. Return the ISO date (|Y| ÷ 100) − (|Y| ÷ 400) − 2 days after the ISO date with the same fields as
    |julian|.

<div class=note>
    These are the algorithms in chapter 8 of [[MEEUS]]. Every other rule in the registry is a media
    query that authors could write themselves; the registry gives them names and keeps regional
    differences, like the length of Passover, in one place.
</div>

New entries are added through the specification's issue tracker. An entry can only be added if its
dates can be calculated. Holidays whose dates depend on observation, or on calendars that
<code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code> doesn't support, aren't eligible; see [[#open-issues]].


<h2 id=privacy>Privacy considerations</h2>

* The current date and time and the user's time zone are already available to script, through
    <code>Date</code>, <code>Temporal</code>, and <code>Intl</code>. These features make them available
    to style sheets, including in contexts where script doesn't run, such as HTML email or pages with
    script blocked. A style sheet can report them to a server by loading different resources under
    different queries. User agents that block script for privacy should consider the same treatment for
    these features, such as evaluating them in the "UTC" time zone.
* The [=current instant=] is rounded down to a second, so queries can't measure the user's clock more
    finely than that.
* The [=hemisphere=] for '@media/season' comes from the time zone, not from the user's location.
* The region in ''holiday()'' is chosen by the author. Nothing in this specification reveals the user's
    region or locale.


<h2 id=security>Security considerations</h2>

* These features show and hide content; they don't protect it. Content hidden until a date is in the
    style sheet and the document already, and users can change their clocks. Embargoed content must stay
    on the server until its release.
* Nothing in this specification fetches resources or runs script.


<h2 id=alternate-proposals>Alternate proposals</h2>

<h3 id=alt-numbers>ISO 8601 basic format as numbers</h3>

ISO 8601's basic format writes dates and times without separators, and those parse as CSS numbers:
<code>(20260909 <= date <= 20260915)</code>, <code>(date = 1225)</code>, <code>(time >= 1800)</code>.
That needs no grammar change. It was not chosen because:

* A number has no time zone or UTC offset, so "9:00 in New York" can't be written.
* A specific moment takes several conditions:
    <code>(date > 20261127), ((date = 20261127) and (time >= 900))</code>.
* Leading zeros vanish, so January 5 is <code>105</code>, and the number of digits decides whether a
    number is a date or a month-day.

<h3 id=alt-features>Features instead of functions</h3>

Holidays and calendars could be media features with no grammar change:
<code>(holiday: us-thanksgiving)</code>, and per-calendar features such as
<code>(hebrew-month: M07) and (hebrew-day >= 15)</code>. It was not chosen because a date can be several
holidays at once, which no other media feature allows, and because a pair of features for every calendar
multiplies the feature names.

<h3 id=alt-locale>A locale in ''holiday()''</h3>

The first argument could be a locale, such as ''en-us''. Holidays depend on the region, not the
language, so only the region is used.


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

<p class=issue>Islamic holidays, such as Eid al-Fitr and the start of Ramadan, begin when the new
crescent moon is sighted, which no algorithm predicts. Authors who accept a tabular approximation can use
<code>calendar(islamic-umalqura, (month: M09))</code>. Should the registry include such entries with that
caveat?

<p class=issue>Diwali, Holi, Vesak, and other holidays of Hindu and Buddhist lunisolar calendars can't
be listed, because CLDR and <code>Intl.DateTimeFormat</code> don't support those calendars.

<p class=issue>Many events are relative to a holiday: Black Friday is the day after US Thanksgiving, and
a sale might run for the week before Christmas. Should ''holiday()'' accept a range of days before and
after?

<p class=issue>Some countries use meteorological seasons, which start on the first of March, June,
September, and December. Should '@media/season' offer them?

<p class=issue>Which days are the weekend depends on the region: Saturday and Sunday in most of the
world, Friday and Saturday in others. Should there be a way to test for the weekend, as
<code>Intl.Locale.prototype.getWeekInfo()</code> reports it?
